{
 "source": "am",
 "accent": "am-ET",
 "status": "ready",
 "version": "1",
 "derives_from": "**There is no published Amharic pronunciation key**, of the kind Bhargava's dictionary gives Hindi or Magay's gives Hungarian -- Amharic lexicography prints no respelling column, because the fidal is a shallow enough orthography not to need one. What this table derives from instead is a real and observable system: **the way Amharic writes a foreign word**, as it is written in Ethiopian newspapers, on signage and in CLDR's own Amharic data. Every device here is read off that: a cluster becomes a run of sixth-order glyphs (ፕላስቲክ, ስፖርት, ኮምፒዩተር, ዋሽንግተን), a foreign velar nasal becomes ንግ (ቦርዲንግ, ሺሊንግ), a falling diphthong takes a coda glide (ታይላንድ, ቻው), and a front rounded vowel takes its rounded back letter (ዙሪክ). Nothing is invented and nothing is borrowed from another table's mechanism except the *idea* of Korean's ㅡ, which is described in tmp/am/build_respell.py.",
 "deviations": "**No new glyph and no mark of any kind.** Every character this table emits is an ordinary letter of the fidal, which is why the legend has nothing to teach. Three distinctions are deliberately lost and each is a property of Amharic orthography rather than of this table.\n\n**Length and gemination are not written.** Amharic geminates phonemically and writes it nowhere -- አለ is both `ala` \"he said\" and `alla` \"there is\" -- so a reader who is handed ፋቶ for Italian /fatːo/ is in exactly the position an Amharic reader is in with an Amharic word. `policy.length` is `none` and `ː` maps to nothing rather than doubling the letter, which in Ethiopic would read as an epenthetic vowel: ፋትቶ is [fatɨto].\n\n**Aspiration and palatalisation are not written**, because the language has neither. Each of the eighteen aspirates and seventeen palatalised consonants the corpus produces has its own rule mapping to the plain letter, rather than a bare `ʰ`/`ʲ` fallback -- that is the Hindi ধন্যবাদ lesson, where `dʰ` fell through to `d` + `ʰ` and `--gaps` could see nothing wrong.\n\n**Labialisation is written as a sequence and not with the labialised forms.** /kwa/ comes out ክዋ where Amharic's own orthography has ኳ. Fusing them would be thirty more fixups and would force `fixups: \"all\"`, and ክዋ is read correctly; ኳስ against ክዋስ is a spelling preference rather than a reading.",
 "approximations": "Eleven merges, and two of them are historical identities rather than approximations at all: **Arabic ح is Ethiopic ሐ and ع is ዐ**, letters an Amharic reader meets in ሐኪም and ሰዓት, and /q/ ق is ቀ, which is how every Amharic transcription of an Arabic name writes it.\n\nThe nine that really are merges: /θ ð/ take ስ ዝ; /ts tsʰ dz/ take ጽ ጅ, so a German z merges with Amharic's own /sʼ/, which is Amharic's merger rather than one introduced here; the retroflex series /ʈ ɖ ɳ ɭ/ takes the plain dentals; Mandarin's six palatal and retroflex sibilants collapse onto ሸ ዠ ቸ ጀ; the front rounded vowels /y ʏ ø œ/ take their rounded back letters ኡ ኦ; /ɯ/ takes እ; and every rhotic -- /r ɾ ɹ ɻ ɽ ʁ/ and Czech's r̝ -- takes ር, because Amharic has one.\n\n/x/ is the one place this table refuses a merge that would have been easy: it takes **ኸ** rather than ሀ, so Russian х and German ch stay clear of /h/. ኸ is the letter Ethiopian Semitic writes a velar fricative with -- Tigrinya ኸ is [x] -- and it is otherwise nearly idle in Amharic, which is what makes it available.",
 "note": "**Ethiopic is the first script in this corpus where a bare consonant is a legal standalone letter, and that removes most of the machinery.** The sixth order (ስ ት ክ) is the bare consonant *and* the [Cɨ] Amharic writes when it spells a foreign cluster, so a consonant needs one rule where every Brahmic table here needs a slot-conditioned three (full letter / joined form / coda), and Klingon's /ɬ/ needs one line rather than a copy of three.\n\nWhat Ethiopic does not have is a vowel *sign*. The vowel is inside the glyph and `spellSlot` appends, so the nucleus cannot write onto the consonant the onset already emitted. The answer is Korean's, arrived at from the other side: Korean writes a loose 초성 and lets the 중성 bind through NFC, and since Ethiopic has no canonical compositions the nucleus here emits the **independent አ-series vowel letter** and 196 generated `syllable_fixups` fuse `<sixth order><vowel letter>` into the single glyph that is both. ፕ + ኣ is ፓ.\n\nThree properties of that shape are why it was chosen over the obvious design, which is 196 onset rules conditioned on `if_nucleus_head`. **An onset cluster needs no lookahead**: every onset consonant emits its sixth order and only the last is adjacent to the vowel letter, so /str/ + /iː/ is ስትሪ and /pl/ + /a/ is ፕላ -- where a forward-looking rule would put the vowel on the *first* consonant, and `spellSlot` cannot see the rest of the onset in any case. **A multi-glyph consonant works for free**, which is what makes /ŋ/ = ንግ and /ŋa/ = ንጋ expressible at all. And **a failed fixup is legible rather than tofu**: the marker is a real letter, so the worst case is a spurious glottal stop (ፕኣ for ፓ) and never a box.\n\n`fixups: \"first\"` is correct as well as cheap: exactly one vowel letter is emitted per syllable and exactly one fixup can match it.\n\n**Three of the four stress devices are dead here and it was checked rather than assumed.** `caps` is dead on a caseless script. `acute` and `grave` are byte-identical no-ops, because `VOWEL_LETTERS` and `ACCENTED` in core/respell.js list Latin, Cyrillic and Greek vowels and no Ethiopic codepoint -- the wall Hebrew, Persian, Tamil, Telugu, Punjabi and Gujarati all hit. `prime` would work and is refused for Tamil's reason: Amharic prominence is not contrastive and is written by neither orthography, so a mark here would be a notation this reader has never met carrying information their own language does not use. `policy.length` is `none` for the reason in `deviations`, and `policy.tone` is `drop` because Amharic has none.",
 "legend": "ሁሉም ፊደል የተለመደ አማርኛ ነው — አዲስ ምልክት የለም። ጭንቀት አይመለከትም።",
